  "answer": "Myopia, or nearsightedness, is a condition where a person can see nearby objects clearly but has difficulty seeing distant objects. This happens because the eye's lens focuses light from distant objects in front of the retina instead of on it. To correct myopia, a concave lens is used. This lens diverges the incoming light rays before they enter the eye, allowing the eye's lens to focus the image correctly on the retina. A ray diagram for myopia correction shows that light rays from a distant object are diverged by the concave lens, making them appear to come from a point closer to the eye, which the eye can then focus on the retina.",
